# Copy to .env.prod on the host and fill in. Never committed.
#
#   sudo install -m 600 -o root -g root .env.prod.example .env.prod && sudoedit .env.prod
#
# Root-owned and 0600, on purpose: the signing key in this file is read by
# compose under sudo (scripts/gc) and by nothing that runs as the deploy user,
# and the deploy refuses a file anyone else can read.
#
# See docs/DEPLOY.md. Eight values must be real, and scripts/deploy.sh refuses
# to deploy while any of them is still a placeholder.

# How you actually reach the box, exactly as you type it in the browser.
# The UI is BUILT with this over in poc-qa-stack, Caddy serves this address,
# and Django derives its allowed host, CSRF origin and cookie flags from it —
# so there is nothing else to keep in agreement with it.
#
#   https://app.example.com   a hostname: Caddy gets a certificate, cookies
#                             are Secure and __Host- prefixed, HSTS is on
#   http://203.0.113.10       a bare IP for a demo: none of the above, and
#                             the deploy says so loudly
PUBLIC_URL=CHANGE_ME_SCHEME_AND_HOST

# The built UI on THIS host, mounted read-only into the runner at /app/ui.
#
# ghostclick is two repositories (docs/BOUNDARY.md). This one holds the runner
# and the control plane and cannot build an app; the app is built in
# poc-qa-stack and deployed here as a directory, on its own clock:
#
#   git clone <poc-qa-stack> /srv/poc-qa-stack
#   cd /srv/poc-qa-stack && npm ci && VITE_AUTH_URL=<PUBLIC_URL> npm run build
#
# VITE_AUTH_URL must be the PUBLIC_URL above. It is baked into the bundle at
# build time, so a UI built without it has no sign-in at all and one built for
# another host signs in somewhere else — and neither shows up as an error
# anywhere. scripts/deploy.sh reads the build and refuses all three cases.
#
# Releasing a new UI is then: rebuild there, `./scripts/gc up -d runner`. The
# runner's own image does not change and does not need to.
GC_WEB_DIR=CHANGE_ME_PATH_TO_ghostclick_web_dist

# The keypair executor tokens are signed with. ONE command prints BOTH lines;
# paste them as printed, single quotes included — a PEM has newlines, and on
# one line they are written as \n, which the quotes keep literal:
#
#   cd auth && python manage.py signing_key --new
#
# The private key goes to the control plane and nowhere else; the runner is
# given the public half only, so a runner that is taken over cannot mint.
# There is no default anywhere in the codebase on purpose: a fallback key is
# one that reaches production unnoticed, because everything keeps working.
GC_SIGNING_KEY=CHANGE_ME_PRIVATE_PEM_FROM_signing_key
GC_AUTH_PUBLIC_KEYS=CHANGE_ME_PUBLIC_KEYS_JSON_FROM_signing_key

# Where the Django admin answers. One path segment, no slashes.
#
# On a box reachable from the internet with no TLS, /admin/ is a login form
# anyone can find and sit in front of — there is no lockout here. This moves it
# somewhere nobody will guess and makes /admin/ a 404. That is obscurity rather
# than a control: it removes the box from every scan that walks the well-known
# paths, and it does not survive the path being screenshotted or shared.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(8).toString('hex'))"
#
GC_ADMIN_PATH=CHANGE_ME_RANDOM_PATH

# Django's own signing key, for sessions and CSRF. Changing it logs everyone out.
#
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME_50_PLUS_RANDOM_CHARS

# The key authenticator-app secrets and recovery codes are encrypted with in
# the database (docs/AUTH.md §12) — deliberately not DJANGO_SECRET_KEY, so a
# database dump and a settings file are two different things to steal. The
# control plane and nowhere else. Losing it makes every enrolled second
# factor unreadable (people re-enrol; nothing else breaks). To rotate, put
# the new key first and the old one after it, comma-separated.
#
#   cd auth && python manage.py mfa_key
GC_MFA_KEY=CHANGE_ME_FERNET_KEY_FROM_mfa_key

# The two stores behind the control plane, on a network nothing else can
# reach. URL-SAFE characters only (letters, digits, - and _), at least 16:
# both are spliced into a URL, and a '@' or '/' would be read as address.
#
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_URL_SAFE
REDIS_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_URL_SAFE

# Who may reach /admin/, as space-separated CIDRs — your office, your VPN.
# Unset, nobody can: the default is an address no packet ever comes from.
# 0.0.0.0/0 is not a value; the deploy's smoke check expects the admin to be
# closed from the box itself.
# GC_ADMIN_CIDRS=203.0.113.0/24 198.51.100.7/32

# Mail. The control plane sends sign-up codes, invitations, reset links and
# the notices for every change to an account, and with DJANGO_DEBUG off it
# refuses to start with SMTP and no host. Until there is a mail host, name the
# console backend and the mail goes to the control plane's log
# (`./scripts/gc logs control`) — say so on purpose, or set the host. With
# the console backend nobody can sign up or reset a password without an
# operator reading the log, so a real deployment sets the host.
DJANGO_EMAIL_BACKEND=django.core.mail.backends.console.EmailBackend
# EMAIL_HOST=smtp.example.com
# EMAIL_PORT=587
# EMAIL_HOST_USER=
# EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD=
# EMAIL_USE_TLS=1
# DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL=ghostclick <no-reply@app.example.com>

# Who may sign up (docs/AUTH.md §4). Unset is `invite`: only an address that
# holds a live invitation — issued by an organisation's owner or admin, and
# mailed to the invitee — can make an account; everyone else is told by mail
# that sign-up is by invitation. `open` lets anyone sign up (put Turnstile in
# front of it, below); `domain` admits exactly the domains listed.
# GC_SIGNUP_MODE=invite
# GC_SIGNUP_DOMAINS=acme.example,globex.example

# Cloudflare Turnstile, from dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile. Both or neither:
# in open mode every sign-up presents a token, and any address that trips the
# failed-sign-in limit (ten a minute) must present one with each sign-in for
# an hour. The site key is public and reaches the UI; the secret stays in the
# control plane. Unset, nothing asks for a challenge.
# GC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# GC_TURNSTILE_SECRET=

# Sign in with Google (docs/AUTH.md §6). An OAuth client from the Google
# Cloud console (APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth client ID, type "Web
# application") whose authorised redirect URI is EXACTLY
#
#   <PUBLIC_URL>/accounts/google/login/callback/
#
# Both or neither; the control plane refuses one without the other. Set,
# "Continue with Google" appears on the sign-in and sign-up pages and
# "Connect a Google account" under Security. Who may make an account
# through it is still GC_SIGNUP_MODE's decision, and in domain mode it is
# the Google Workspace (the id_token's hd) that must match, not the
# address. Unset, nothing about Google exists.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The recorder extension (docs/AUTH.md §11). With a login, the extension's
# "Send to ghostclick" is handed an executor token by the control plane on
# the strength of the person's session, and only an extension listed here
# is: its origin is chrome-extension://<id>, one id per install, and the
# panel shows the one to paste (under "Where ghostclick is running").
# Comma-separated; both services read this line. Unset, the recorder can
# only Copy mermaid.
# GC_EXTENSION_ORIGINS=chrome-extension://abcdefghijklmnopabcdefghijklmnop

# Optional --------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long an executor token lives. 120 to 600 seconds; the deploy refuses
# more. Not below 120: the UI renews with a minute still on the clock, so a
# shorter life means every call mints and the mint limit answers 429.
# GC_TOKEN_TTL=600

# The limits that are this project's rather than allauth's: issuing an
# invitation (the one endpoint that mails a stranger), passkey sign-in (the
# one WebAuthn endpoint an anonymous caller reaches), minting an executor token
# (per session) and accepting an invitation (per address). Defaults are
# sensible; set them only to tighten. A value that does not read as a rate
# stops the control plane at startup.
# GC_INVITE_RATE=20/h/user
# GC_PASSKEY_LOGIN_RATE=10/m/ip
# GC_MINT_RATE=12/10m
# GC_ACCEPT_RATE=10/m/ip

# How much of a run is performed for a watcher (docs: GC_PACE_MS). 0 is as fast
# as the page allows; 420 is the laptop default. 250 is a reasonable remote
# middle — brisk, still followable over a screencast.
GC_PACE_MS=250
GC_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
GC_SETTLE_MS=250

# Automatic fixes (README: "Automatic fixes (GC_HEAL)"). off (the default) runs
# exactly as before. safe lets a run carry on past harmless differences from the
# recording — a cookie or newsletter layer dismissed by its reject/close button,
# an element that arrived late, a list the recording never opened, a label that
# became a placeholder of the same name — and never renames a target or drops a
# scope. ai adds Claude for what the rules could not fix, behind guards, at most
# GC_HEAL_AI_MAX_CALLS calls a run and GC_HEAL_AI_MAX_CALLS_PER_DAY calls a day
# per organisation (in memory; a restart resets it), and only for an
# organisation whose owner or admin has opted in. A word the runner does not
# know stops it at boot.
# GC_HEAL=safe
# GC_HEAL_AI_MAX_CALLS=6
# GC_HEAL_AI_MAX_CALLS_PER_DAY=100
#
# The key for ai. The runner reads it at boot, keeps it out of the browser's
# environment and never prints it; without it ai runs as safe and says so.
# The runner then makes HTTPS requests to api.anthropic.com: it is on the
# `edge` network only, which is an ordinary bridge with a route out (the one
# its browser already uses to reach the sites under test), so no compose change
# is needed — but a host firewall or egress proxy must allow that host. Use a
# key of its own, with a spend limit: it sits in the container that drives
# pages other people choose.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# One browser, one organisation driving it at a time (docs/AUTH.md §10). After
# an organisation's run has ended and none of its viewers has done anything
# for this long, the browser is free for the next organisation; until then
# they are told the runner is busy. Milliseconds; a minute unless changed.
# GC_RUNNER_IDLE_MS=60000

# Switches (docs/HARDENING.md): parts of the product turned off for everyone,
# with no deploy. Comma-separated: a switch, a service (`runner` is every
# runner.* switch, `control` every control.* one), or * for all of them.
#   runner.recording   runner.runs   runner.onboarding   runner.origins
#   runner.driving     runner.heal      control.signup   control.invitations
#   control.google     control.passkeys
# Both services read this line, and a name either one does not know stops it
# at boot. The control plane's admin switches them too, without a restart.
# GC_SWITCHES_OFF=

# Request limits (docs/HARDENING.md), per client address as the edge reports
# it. The runner counts in its own process; the control plane counts in Redis
# beside allauth's sign-in limits. The defaults are shown; set one only to
# change it.
#
# The runner: every /api request; bad tokens before an address is shut out
# for the rest of the window; socket tickets; socket connections; and
# messages per socket.
# GC_API_RATE=600/m
# GC_AUTH_FAIL_RATE=20/5m
# GC_TICKET_RATE=30/m
# GC_WS_CONNECT_RATE=30/m
# GC_WS_MESSAGE_RATE=3000/10s
#
# The control plane: every request, and GET /auth/csrf on its own, since
# that is the one that starts a session for anyone who asks.
# GC_REQUEST_RATE=300/m/ip
# GC_CSRF_RATE=60/m/ip

# The log (docs/HARDENING.md). Both services answer every request with an
# X-Request-Id and log under it. A request is logged when its traceparent is
# sampled — the UI's "Trace my requests" — unless GC_REQUEST_LOG says all, or
# off. Compose writes JSON lines, which is what json-file keeps.
# GC_LOG_LEVEL=info
# GC_REQUEST_LOG=sampled

# Vault values, if a suite needs them. Named GC_SECRET_<NAME>; the value never
# leaves the server and is redacted out of the forwarded browser console.
# With auth on the environment feeds only the `local` organisation, which no
# token can act for: a real organisation's vault is the file
# .ghostclick/<org>/secrets.json in the runner's state volume, so that one
# tenant's secret is never every tenant's.
# GC_SECRET_QA_PASS=
